<?php
/**
 * Spartrak — the add-to-cart success message, as the THREE parts Figma's
 * alert draws.
 *
 * FIGMA SSOT: 1286:29513 ("Alert - Dark", success). It states a title, a
 * supporting line and an action:
 *
 *     Pistol 120mm Added to Cart
 *     View your cart and checkout now!
 *     [ View cart ]
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHY THIS FILE IS WHERE THE STRUCTURE COMES FROM
 * ===========================================================================
 * A Magento message is ONE string. Core emits `.message > div > <text>` on
 * both of its message pipelines, so a stylesheet alone cannot split a
 * sentence into a title, a body and a control — there is nothing in the markup
 * to address.
 *
 * Core does support structured messages, through complex-message renderers,
 * and add-to-cart is one of them: `Magento\Checkout\Controller\Cart\Add` calls
 * `addComplexSuccessMessage('addCartSuccessMessage', [...])`, and
 * Magento_Checkout's own frontend di.xml points that code at THIS template
 * through BlockRenderer. Overriding the template is therefore the platform's
 * own extension point for exactly this — no plugin, no observer, no fork of a
 * controller, and the message keeps flowing through the same session and the
 * same message manager it always did.
 *
 * The stock template this replaces reads:
 *
 *     You added %1 to your <a href="%2">shopping cart</a>.
 *
 * — one sentence with a link buried in the middle of it, in English on an
 * Arabic storefront because it was never translated. The three strings below
 * are new source strings and are translated in this theme's i18n/ar_EG.csv.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * THE MARKUP IS CONSTRAINED BY MAGENTO'S OWN JS ESCAPER
 * ===========================================================================
 * On the Knockout path this HTML is passed through
 * `Magento_Theme/js/view/messages::prepareMessageForHtml`, which runs
 * Magento_Security/js/escaper with `allowedTags: ['div','span','b','strong',
 * 'i','em','u','a']`. Attributes are filtered separately, and `class` and
 * `href` are both in that escaper's `generallyAllowedAttributes`.
 *
 * So: spans and one anchor, carrying classes and an href, and nothing else.
 * No <p>, no <button>, no data-* attributes — any of those would survive the
 * server-rendered path and be silently stripped on the AJAX one, which is the
 * worst kind of difference to debug.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * ESCAPING
 * ===========================================================================
 * The product name is merchant data and goes through escapeHtml; the cart URL
 * through escapeUrl. The surrounding markup is a literal in this file, which
 * is why it is written out rather than passed through escapeHtml with an
 * allow-list — nothing dynamic is interpolated into it.
 *
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */

$productName = (string) $block->getData('product_name');
$cartUrl = (string) $block->getData('cart_url');
?>
<span class="spartrak-toast__text"><span class="spartrak-toast__title"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('%1 added to your cart', $productName)) ?></span><span class="spartrak-toast__body"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('View your cart and checkout now!')) ?></span></span><a class="spartrak-toast__action" href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($cartUrl) ?>"><?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('View cart')) ?></a>
